WEBVTT

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:03.930
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:14.700
 So we made these baked tamales called geta vengi, and so it's a took recipe from the isthmus,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:23.850
 and it's kind of nixtamalized corn that's been browned with lard and stuffed with chile and dried shrimp head mole.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:29.910
 Yeah, they're kind of like, they taste like cornbread prepared.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.130
 Just a lot of, like, things they find on the ranch, you know, that like the dogs suddenly bring in.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:40.850
 They prepare a lot of armadillo and iguana, lots of things using dried chiles.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.810
 They use a chile costeño, shrimp, corn, wild herbs, crab.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:54.170
 So it just got me thinking a lot about how to get people engaged here and to other questions

00:00:54.170 --> 00:01:00.070
 of what's happening in the Mexican countryside and the issues that are important to farmers.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:06.500
 So those are things that I'm still sitting with and trying to understand how, how to, in some ways,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:13.890
 create encounters or create experiences that, that help burst the bubble a little bit.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:23.600
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Chelsea Wills and me, Devin Sampson.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:31.540
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists,

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.940
 and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.620
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.580
 and who have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.650
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app,

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.910
 and you can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:02:02.980
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.950
 I'm Chelsea Wills, and I'm here today interviewing Nikki Nakazawa.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.460
 Nikki is a chef based in Mexico City.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.380
 We met a few years ago at an artist residency in rural Michoacan.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:22.150
 Since moving to Mexico City in 2007, Nikki Nakazawa has navigated between art, architecture, music, and food worlds.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.110
 After several years working as a managing editor at art and architecture publishing houses,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:35.630
 she founded the experimental pop-up restaurant and catering company, Pichon, with Emma Rosenbush and Kenny Curran.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.690
 Pichon is a pop-up restaurant and project dedicated to culinary research and experimentation.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:49.150
 It is inspired by the chinampas of Mexico City, the culinary traditions of the Mexican countryside,

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.470
 and the gastronomic revolution that has transformed food culture.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.540
 They believe that the best food is prepared with ingredients, grown locally and sustainably.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.710
 They believe that food should be a vehicle for strengthening community from its cultivation to its consumption.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:17.120
 The experiment began in 2012 and continues to produce culinary experiences that connect diners to an exploration of what it means to eat local in Mexico City.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.970
 Hi, Nikki. Nice to be talking to you today.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:21.370
 Hi.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.330
 Welcome to the show.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:24.970
 Thanks again for the invitation.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.090
 Of course.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:34.370
 So, I just wanted to start by hearing about how you started cooking and what drew you to food.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:37.730
 Let's see.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:42.810
 I guess I've always cooked since I was a kid.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.070
 My mom was not a big cook.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.970
 She never seemed to have time to do it.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:54.050
 She worked from home, but kind of had her hands full with my two sisters and I.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:04:10.120
 So, I started cooking after school and making my own meals and I always really liked it and kind of spent a lot of time hanging out with my grandparents in the kitchens and seeing what they were up to there.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:19.600
 So, it was something that started as a kid and I guess I started professionally three years ago, a little over three years ago when I started Pichon.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.380
 Okay, well, let's go back.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.800
 Let's talk about before you were a professional cook.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:35.900
 I started with a kind of brunch project informally when I was in college.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:48.540
 My roommates were really amazing cooks as well and one of my roommates in particular, her older sister, was like super into throwing these kind of elaborate dinner parties.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:05:07.340
 And so, we kind of inherited this incredible pantry of just like crazy random goods, everything from rose water to like all sorts of like Asian spices and Indian spices and everything.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.860
 We just had a super amazing pantry to play with.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:30.100
 So, my roommates and I were all kind of like a multicultural crew, a girl from Seattle, a woman from New York, but a Uzbek background and a Mexican-American girl and me.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:38.380
 And so, we just started kind of making these meals together and inviting our friends to hang out and drink and eat all day.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:46.790
 So, I guess that you could say that that was the genesis of my thinking about food and bringing people together.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:53.830
 Well, I only ask that because I feel like a big part of your story is like you were raised in the U.S.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:06:01.810
 And you live in Mexico City and I feel like I've only eaten with you and I've only eaten your food a few times.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:09.700
 But I always feel like there's this part about it where there's this collision of worlds that's always happening that you seem to like.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:11.680
 Yeah.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:18.210
 And that, I don't know, that feels like that fits into that story to me.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.210
 And maybe you could talk about how Pichon started and, yeah, what brought that about?

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:37.230
 Pichon started a little bit earlier than what we usually say is our official start date, which was May 2012.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:56.140
 This is because it was an idea that Emma Rosenbush and I had initially together and then coincided with a project of two architect friends of mine, Damien Figueras and Memorri de Teresa.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:07:12.480
 So these are two friends of mine who they had space in mind in Roma and had already started kind of negotiating on this space and had an idea of putting this kind of cafe, cultural cafe together.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:25.840
 And so while they were having this idea, we had the idea that we wanted to have a more food driven concept because they were architects and thinking about all the other stuff that was going to happen there, but not the food.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:35.080
 So one day talking to Memorri de Damien, they were like, oh, we have this idea for cafe and, you know, maybe we could join.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:43.580
 And so we were actually working on, you know, Pichon for something like eight months before we started on the pop-up.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:55.940
 We were like around seven partners and had kind of been negotiating on the space for a really long time and just ended up being super complicated.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:15.250
 And the budget started getting larger and larger and larger and, um, and me, Emma and Kenny kind of realized, you know what, I think that this is maybe getting a little out of hand and we, we just need to, to move on this and start doing something, even if it's not going to end up being a brick and mortar establishment.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:31.830
 And so at that point, um, we decided that we were going to try out the pop-up model, which at that point was kind of becoming popular in places like, uh, London and New York and other cities where rents were super high.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:47.080
 Um, so there's this little Fonda, um, a couple of blocks from our house and Emma went eat there one weekend or one weekday and discovered that they closed on Sundays.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:54.270
 And so she asked whether they would be willing to rent us the space on, on a weekend and they said yes.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:04.860
 And so we were able to kind of, we had to do our, our first, uh, for something like, uh, $20, $20 rent.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.420
 And that's how it began.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:14.840
 That's a, that's a pretty good story that you kind of went in a big circle to start.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.540
 It sounded like, um, and initially you were doing Pichon once a month.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:20.920
 Is that right?

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:22.150
 Yeah.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:22.870
 Yeah.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:30.550
 We started out once a month and I think that was the during two months and then a pretty quickly.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.570
 It escalated to like twice a month.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:37.860
 And then it was every week, um, for about a year.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:44.640
 Um, and that was because at our first pop-up event in that little Fonda,

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:52.820
 um, two of our friends came, um, again, an architect friend of mine who had started another

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.200
 little cafe in, in San Miguel, Chapultepec called Cafe Sena.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:01.970
 And it was him and a group of architects, designers who had put together the space.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:09.850
 And again, really considered the food concept had, had, had an understanding about the potential

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:14.470
 of a space to be a platform for a lot of different types of activities.

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:19.710
 But, you know, the, the, the part about the actual day-to-day operations and what it would

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:28.090
 mean, uh, they would be serving people would not be, uh, solidified or understand, understandable

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:28.570
 to them.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:34.990
 And so, um, they were like, well, you know, we, we want to be open seven days a week.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:41.540
 And on Sundays, if we have to hire people, then we'll have to pay them double or we'll

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.840
 have to have like a separate group of staff.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:48.360
 So maybe that's a good day to, to do this kind of takeover.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:53.550
 How did the three of you find yourself in Mexico city at the same time?

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:57.090
 And how did you and Kenny and Emma come together and start cooking?

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:02.440
 Well, I, I guess I, I was the first one to work in Mexico city.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.780
 I've been here now since November of 2007.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.290
 So I'm coming up on my eight year anniversary.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:18.660
 It's kind of a long story, uh, how I arrived here, but, um, just kind of in a nutshell,

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:26.300
 I, um, was a student at university of Chicago in 2005, um, did a little program in Oaxaca,

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:33.020
 uh, then, and just totally fell in love and hadn't really known anything about Mexico before

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:42.400
 coming and started studying up and decided I was going to come in 2007 and see, and see

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:43.940
 what I could do here.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:48.520
 Um, I thought it might be a year and it just kept on, it's kept on staying and staying.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:52.940
 Kenny came in a more random fashion.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:59.800
 Um, his boyfriend, PJ Roundtree, who's another member of Pichon, but what just wasn't one of

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:04.190
 the founding members, but he was a part of Pichon for a long time.

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:17.160
 Um, he was, uh, he went to RISD along with Kenny, um, and they were living apart for a while,

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:24.640
 uh, one in Philly and the other in New Orleans and decided, and PJ kind of had his fascination

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.540
 with Mexico city and, and decided that they were just going to come and figure it out.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:34.110
 Uh, and so Kenny just moved to Mexico city.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:47.020
 I think this must've been in 2010, 2010, um, with his cat and didn't know any Spanish and

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:48.360
 just sorted it out.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:56.540
 Uh, and so he, uh, was, he's a painter by trade, well, by training and then, uh, has been cooking

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.340
 in professional kitchens for the past 10 years.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:04.230
 Uh, Emma was traveling throughout Latin America.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:07.910
 Um, she had saved some money.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:11.430
 She was working at, uh, an attorney's office.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:18.860
 She was doing, um, some prison law work and, uh, some prison advocacy stuff.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:24.920
 And so she, uh, decided that she wanted to travel around Latin America for six months.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:30.200
 And so she was coming through Mexico and I had met her in college at some point.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:35.120
 And, um, she and I linked up again.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:41.420
 We totally just loved each other again and decided that we wanted to work on a project together.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.730
 And so I kind of convinced her to, to stick around Mexico city and see whether we could make

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:47.290
 something happen.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:53.740
 Well, let's talk a little bit about Mexico city and why it's such an interesting place

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.060
 to do something like this.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:59.940
 I know that you have worked with some, of some farmers in the Southern part of the city.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.530
 And you know that you've done a lot of different things in the time that you've lived there.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:06.550
 You've worked with artists and architects and musicians.

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:11.250
 And it seems like these food projects now, they're like these moments of coalescence in a lot

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:11.710
 of ways.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:12.850
 What does that feel like?

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:16.240
 Um, that was like five questions in one.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:17.220
 Yeah.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:19.260
 I'm trying to see where to begin right here.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:30.100
 Uh, I mean, Mexico city has the great heart or like the great fortune of being, um, an incredible,

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.080
 like incredibly dynamic place.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:38.760
 So I mean, there's a lot of things happening at once in a lot of different worlds, um, you

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:47.540
 can navigate within, um, that has a dark side as well, which, um, is the fact that it's an

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.140
 incredibly class divided society.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:53.430
 Uh, and part of that dynamism is actually a result of inequality.

00:14:53.820 --> 00:15:01.570
 Um, so I think that one of these, one of the magical feelings that you have when you first

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:06.590
 come to Mexico city is the sense that, wow, everybody connected and you end up in these

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:16.660
 little worlds and, and you get caught in this network feeling, uh, where, uh, yeah, everybody

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:25.540
 knows everybody and it's all connected to this global scene and, uh, it's exciting and there's

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:31.490
 all, and you, and you get kind of this look into both the high and the low of, of what

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:32.450
 happens in Mexico city.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:39.990
 Um, so that's, that's, uh, an exciting and a really beautiful thing, but it also, uh, in

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:46.630
 some ways, um, observes a lot of the other things that are happening in Mexico that aren't

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:54.530
 that little magical little privilege web, uh, which is, you know, the reality of, uh, the

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:58.370
 Mexican countryside, which is the reality of drugs, which is really.

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:02.850
 There's a lot of other things that are, that are very real and that are very much happening

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:04.220
 at the same time.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:13.990
 Um, so I think that for me, having been in some ways, uh, privy to this very, very privileged

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:24.540
 space in Mexico city, working in galleries and in publishing and, um, having, um, a direct

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:34.340
 connection or link to all these super global figures, um, I was, uh, I, I guess I didn't

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:40.910
 know it super consciously, but then was looking for it, but looking for a way to be connected

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:43.410
 to what all the other things that we're having in Mexico.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:52.020
 So I think that actually going to farms, going out, leaving the city and leaving the little

00:16:52.020 --> 00:17:00.290
 bubble in which we are constantly just reinforcing our own class values was important and, um, very

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:02.130
 eyeopening for me.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:11.700
 Um, so, so yeah, so we, we started working initially with, um, some friends who started

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:18.000
 this, uh, farming project in the Chinampas of Xochimilco.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:22.610
 So this is the Southern area of Mexico city.

00:17:22.610 --> 00:17:29.950
 It's a canal system that, uh, I think it's about 200 kilometers of navigable canals.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:38.380
 Um, and it's a crazy living archeological ruin.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:44.970
 So for, there's the many, many reasons that this was extremely interesting us.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:51.510
 And one of them being that 80% of these chinos are in some state of disuse.

00:17:51.750 --> 00:18:01.010
 So the utopic like dimensions or possibility, uh, was tremendous and is tremendous still.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.760
 Can you describe what they are for people who don't know what a Chinampas is?

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:07.520
 Sure.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:11.910
 So this is a pre-Hispanic farming technique.

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:15.230
 Um, I guess it dates back to almost 7,000 years ago.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:24.590
 Um, they were agricultural islands that were formed by sinking boats or reed structures and

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:31.330
 using the, the mud from the bottom of the canals to sink them and then, um, inter kind of mixing

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:37.170
 them with, with a lot of kind of organic material and, and hay and whatever they found.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:43.100
 And, um, and then after they built them up enough, what they would do is plant these willow

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:49.330
 trees called out with this, and these trees would, they have a specific type of root structure

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.150
 with, which would compact the earth together.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:58.130
 Um, and so these were, this is one of the earliest systems of, uh, intended irrigation.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:07.570
 And, um, Nesawakoyot, who was the Nahuatl, uh, emperor and leader in the 1500s.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:18.100
 He was, um, slightly pre-conquest, uh, he had created a system of dams in the lake system,

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:25.050
 which protected and sheltered this area from the salty waters of the, the northern part of

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:33.070
 lake local, but once the Spanish came, they started taking down all these, um, this system

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:36.790
 of lovies and dams and, and they kind of, and they started draining the water.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:41.050
 And so this system was lost for a period.

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:47.770
 And then, um, over the course of hundreds of years, there's been various regimens of water

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:57.350
 in Mexico City in which they drained the water and then, uh, in two-weighted or put the, the

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:03.010
 underground springs and rivers that feed this canal system, water system.

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:12.270
 And, and so it's, it's a long story, but basically they're agricultural islands that date back.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:20.060
 And I, for me, whenever I've been there, I always sort of imagine them as, um, they don't,

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:21.150
 they're not floating.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:26.830
 They are actually like islands built in a lake, but they're always being constructed, right?

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:31.120
 So they're always being, there's always soil being added to the top.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:38.240
 And there are these, they're incredibly fertile, dense, little agricultural places that are

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:44.110
 managed in like all of these different ways that are as unique as all the different people

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:44.790
 who live there.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.890
 Right.

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:51.160
 So, um, what are some of the things that go ahead?

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:51.600
 Yeah.

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:58.110
 No, that there, there, it's not, um, there aren't a whole lot of people living right there.

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:04.190
 I mean, there's some people living on the edges of the canals, but, um, but it's mostly

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:12.860
 used for, there's recreational areas now and, uh, areas for growing ornamental plants.

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:19.200
 And then it's kind of a, an area where there's these types of gondola boats that go around

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:26.180
 and kids, teenagers just can, can get together, play music and get drunk on the boats.

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:31.640
 There's a, lots of birthday parties and things like that, but there's, there's a bunch of

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:35.940
 different, um, ports or little docks that you can leave from.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:42.880
 And so each dock has its own characteristics and some are more commercial and others are

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:51.120
 more touristic and others are more focused on, on nature and, and rowing and other types

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:52.180
 of outdoor activities.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:58.230
 So how did you meet the farmers that you work with as part of Petron?

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:03.870
 You started working with farmers who farm on the Chinampas.

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:05.280
 Is that part of that story?

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:07.400
 How, what, what is that story?

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:16.330
 Well, this is a friend of my friend Memo was the architect and, uh, he was in touch with

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:22.400
 these two kids who decided that they were going to start this, um, organic farming project

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:23.340
 on the Chinampas.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.220
 So, um, we connected with them.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:31.640
 And so they were the ones who had started linking up with different farmers there, which for them,

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:38.180
 I know was a real challenge because there aren't, there aren't a whole lot of people who continue

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:45.030
 to farm in the traditional way and, and such a local, a lot of them are using chemicals.

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:53.490
 Um, and then, uh, economically it's just not the best thing to do within the city.

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:59.410
 If you're in the city to be a farmer for a lot of young people seems totally ridiculous

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:02.460
 because you can probably earn more doing something else.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:08.860
 So, uh, the, the, the type of people or the type of, yeah, the type of folks who decide

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:10.480
 that they're going to farm in Mexico city.

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:15.640
 It's a, it's a, it's a particular brand of stubborn, idealistic folk.

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:26.820
 So, um, so, uh, Lucio Antonio were, were the, the two people behind the company.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:34.400
 Yolkan have done a, an amazing job really seeking out people who, who really want to work in

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:35.930
 this way and see the value in it.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:39.030
 So you were just in Oaxaca.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:44.870
 You just did a residency outside of Puerto Escondido at a place called Casa Wabi, right?

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:47.890
 And were you working with farmers there?

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:55.000
 And let's, let's, let's talk about farmers and food traditions and what makes sense and

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:58.020
 what doesn't make sense and how all those things happen at the same time.

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:02.000
 You got interested in working with farmers and where food came from.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:06.550
 It sounds like before you started working with the farmers in the Chinampas, but then where

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:07.150
 did that go?

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:09.890
 I know that you're doing more work out of Mexico city.

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:17.910
 Well, I mean, one of the other things that happened while we were starting embarking on the

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:25.070
 Pichon project was that, um, one of the first local organic markets started up in Mexico city.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:30.130
 It's called Mercado El Cien, El Cien referring to 100 kilometers.

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:38.660
 So that means that all the farmers who are represented in this small farmers market, um, come from

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:41.920
 places that are around a hundred kilometers from Mexico city.

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:45.530
 So that gave us a place to start.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:53.290
 Um, so a lot of the farmers that we continue to work with, um, our contacts that were made

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.590
 through that, um, network.

00:24:55.790 --> 00:25:05.700
 And, um, a lot of them are in Estado de Mexico or the state of Mexico outside of, um, the DF.

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:14.210
 And then some are in Moreno, some are in Hidalgo and the way that we work with them is generally,

00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:21.830
 I mean, it's pretty direct and we're, we're kind of just, um, since we're a pop-up and we're

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:29.140
 not as regular as, uh, a restaurant or any other type of food project, really, it allows us to,

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:31.440
 to spend a lot of time hanging out.

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:39.370
 So we, our relationships have often begun just going out to the farms and hanging out

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:44.270
 and seeing what they're doing and, and starting and finding ways that we can be more sensitive

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:51.790
 or, or, uh, understand how they work and what products they need to get off their hands

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:56.870
 or what products they have, uh, in volume at certain types of times of the year.

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:05.860
 And it's, it's been a project of sensitizing ourselves to, to their, um, I mean, I certainly

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:10.760
 never had any type of background in farming or in agriculture.

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:15.460
 So this has been a huge ongoing lesson for me.

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:25.350
 Um, and, uh, especially understanding the way that, uh, Mexican traditional farmers work

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:33.270
 depending on the crops and depending on the geography of the place, uh, that they're, that they're

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:33.730
 dealing with.

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:37.470
 So a lot of these farmers farm in traditional milta settings.

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:44.600
 So, um, there are smaller plots and they actually are completely, um, uncommercial in the sense

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:48.400
 that they often put more money into, to growing them.

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:52.390
 They actually can potentially earn from, from, from it.

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:59.870
 Uh, so they're, so they're farming for their own consumption, essentially.

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:02.970
 And what do they grow?

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:10.810
 Uh, in the milpa, they're growing corn, beans, squash, chiles.

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:16.020
 And you're buying things some of the time.

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:18.890
 I mean, do you have like a, what does it look like?

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:23.250
 Do you have a lot of relationships with a lot of different farmers that you buy things from?

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:31.180
 Um, uh, we, we do, um, but we're generally buying, um, from the, our farmers in the Chinampa.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:36.350
 They'll send out a list of what they have available every week and then we'll, you know, look through

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:36.610
 that.

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:44.220
 And then we work with an organic farmer in Cuautla and Morelos and, uh, we have, we'll go

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:47.520
 to the organic market on the weekend and see what's available.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:53.230
 So, you know, it, it can get pretty broad for certain types of things, but like generally

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:57.570
 we, we stick with around four or five providers.

00:27:58.010 --> 00:27:58.640
 Cool.

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:01.380
 Let's talk about Oaxaca.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:02.900
 What'd you do there?

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:05.160
 What was that project?

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:14.970
 Uh, so, um, I spent, yeah, I spent a little per month and, um, at Casa Wabi, which is this

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:22.750
 artist residency, just 30 months outside Puerto Escondido towards, um, Rio Grande, so towards

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.390
 the North, the North, I guess.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:34.190
 Um, and my intention was to do a small research project and document some of the recipes from

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:34.810
 the coast.

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:37.920
 Um, and, um, that's what I started doing.

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:42.600
 I, I, uh, was lucky enough to have my boyfriend's actually from Puerto Escondido.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:50.860
 So one of his childhood friends, um, offered his mother-in-law to me and, uh, I went over

00:28:50.860 --> 00:29:00.060
 to their house and she had this great brick, um, oven that her son built and, um, uh, like

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:06.870
 all this wood burning, um, stoves and ovens and places to play.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:11.380
 And so we started, uh, she started teaching me some, some traditional recipes from the

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:19.330
 Isness of Tawantepec and, uh, uh, some other, uh, recipes from the, um, she's Sapotec, so

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:21.430
 some traditional Sapotec recipes.

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:28.670
 And then I, uh, ended up cooking with a relative who's from the, the Afro-Mestiso area of the

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:28.970
 coast.

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:35.410
 And we, we cooked some traditional recipes that she knew and, um, just spent a lot of time

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:39.390
 hanging out and cooking and shopping and, and it was a lot of fun.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:48.150
 So, um, I, I basically got the beginnings of, of what might be a little recipe collection

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:52.800
 of a lot of these women's, um, uh, recipes.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:57.180
 And, um, there's still a lot of research to be done just because it's really hard to parse

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:04.390
 what, where things are from, because a lot of the recipes are, are, are kind of things

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:10.390
 that are repeated in a lot of the communities along the coast, but that have slight variations.

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:18.680
 Um, so are you trying to figure out where things were from as part of what you were looking

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:18.920
 into?

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:25.060
 Um, and so, yeah, in some ways, and, and, and the things that, you know, also I, uh, I

00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:31.960
 couldn't, I didn't come to the problem with this huge, um, I guess, ambition to do like

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:37.430
 this really like in-depth project just because also so much of everything has to do with building

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:40.110
 relationships initially and kind of getting a lay of the land.

00:30:40.270 --> 00:30:44.910
 And this is the first time that I was spending, um, any time in this particular part of the

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:48.760
 coast, um, and getting to know what was happening there.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:55.050
 So it was, it was, it was a, it was a way to stick my toe in to see what, what, what's

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:59.830
 going on there and learn a little bit more about the history of, of, um, of the coast and

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:05.410
 the relationship between the different, um, indigenous communities and, um, the Afro-Mastisa

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:05.910
 community.

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:11.540
 And so this is something that, um, is still greatly, um, understudied.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:18.350
 So I think that if anything, what I learned is that, um, a lot of work has to be done there

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:20.190
 and a lot of research has done there.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:26.640
 And then you can start maybe creating a genealogy or understanding some of the recipes and whether

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:34.450
 they have any origins in, uh, in Western Africa or, um, or, you know, just the, the relationship

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:42.160
 between, um, just all these different communities that, that had, were isolated, um, during many

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:46.580
 years until the, the, uh, first house was built in the seventies.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:50.030
 I love the idea of this, of a genealogy of recipes.

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:53.880
 I think that's such a good description of how things are actually shared.

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.750
 What, what are some of the things you cooked out there?

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:05.280
 Um, so we made, uh, these baked, baked tamales called geta vingi.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:12.180
 And so it's a product recipe from the isthmus and, uh, it's, um, kind of nixtamalized corn

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:24.470
 that then we ground, uh, with lard and, uh, they're stuffed with this, um, chile and, um, dried

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:33.630
 shrimp head mole and, um, and also stuffed with, um, these dried, uh, river shrimp.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:40.830
 So, yeah, these, they're, they're, they're kind of like, they, they taste like, um, like

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:41.550
 cornbread.

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:43.840
 It tastes like cornbread in a way because they're baked.

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:49.240
 So it's a little bit drier, but has that, uh, yeah, a lot of, I think a lot of preparation

00:32:49.240 --> 00:33:00.890
 with the shrimp, um, did some yellow moles, um, prepared, uh, tejon, which I think, I forget

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:02.530
 what it is, what it is in English.

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:03.430
 I'll have to look it up.

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:09.210
 Um, but, um, just a lot of like things that they find on the ranch, you know,

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:13.570
 that like the dogs suddenly bring in, they, they prepare a lot of arm, uh, armadillo

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:14.610
 and iguana.

00:33:15.710 --> 00:33:20.770
 Um, we made, um, chipil, tamales of chipil.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:24.780
 Chipil is a kind of wild herb, what they call calites.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:29.540
 So, um, it's kind of like a, an alfalfa.

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:34.430
 So lots of, lots of things using dried chilies.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:43.350
 They say they use a chile costeño, um, shrimp, corn, uh, wild herbs, um, crab.

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:45.820
 Sounds, sounds delicious.

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:48.040
 It's making me hungry to hear about it.

00:33:48.890 --> 00:33:49.910
 Yeah.

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:56.150
 What was it like to learn a place through its food or to, yeah.

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:57.990
 I mean, what was that like as a starting point?

00:33:58.070 --> 00:34:08.530
 Uh, well, I think something that was interesting to me is I, uh, I spent a lot of time in the

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:09.630
 kitchen with women.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:16.590
 So I, I suppose that since I always go to what's deal with my boyfriend and his family and he

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:25.300
 has three brothers, um, I've always entered into this male space in, on the coast.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:32.660
 And now, um, doing this project that was mostly around cooking, entering into people's family

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:35.480
 spaces, and then also into like exclusively female spaces.

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:41.410
 I had an experience in some time and the types of conversations that happened there and the

00:34:41.410 --> 00:34:42.010
 intimities.

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:47.130
 And, uh, that was, that was, that was really interesting to me.

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:58.810
 And, um, and I, and I guess I hadn't really understood that firsthand, um, since I, well, yeah.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:01.710
 Since I had my first contact with, with a coast.

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:03.810
 So that was, that was really cool.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:10.970
 Um, and, uh, and also I think that it's something, you know, food is something that people get

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:16.210
 really excited to talk about and, and everybody can, can go on and on.

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:20.490
 I mean, Mexico in general, I think like if you ask anybody what they like to eat or what

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:25.780
 their, their parents prepare, what they prepare their families, uh, you know, celebrations,

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:28.550
 it's something that we can get really excited about.

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:33.700
 So, uh, that was, it was, it's a very kind of generous space to enter into.

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:36.810
 That's such a nice description of that.

00:35:36.950 --> 00:35:41.970
 I, I think one of the things I love about food is that everyone's an expert and everybody

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:42.770
 has an opinion.

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:44.850
 Uh, yeah.

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:46.070
 It's so easy that way.

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:48.100
 You know, everyone has a lot to say.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:54.950
 How do you, how does that work come back to the work that you're doing with Pichon back

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:55.750
 in Mexico City?

00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:05.000
 Um, well, in a, in a very almost superficial way, you could say that, uh, I'm now, you

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:12.280
 know, I have events coming up and I'm going to pull upon some of those recipes and the things

00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:16.890
 that I was taught while I was there to just, you know, for a difference.

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:20.890
 And so I'm going to make some of the tamales that we prepared, um, but with like my own

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:21.550
 little twist.

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:27.880
 And so that's the, that's the kind of the first level that, uh, I'll start pulling from

00:36:27.880 --> 00:36:31.260
 that information or, or, or that inspiration.

00:36:31.930 --> 00:36:36.010
 Um, and then, and then, I don't know, I don't know.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:45.640
 I get, it just got me thinking a lot about what, um, about how to get people engaged here

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:52.550
 and, and to other questions of what's happening in the Mexican countryside and the issues that

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:59.350
 are important to agriculture, uh, to, um, farmers, and it's just agricultores.

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:09.630
 Um, and, uh, so those are things that I'm still sitting with and trying to understand

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:17.900
 how, how to, in some ways create encounters or create experiences that, that help burst

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:18.940
 the bubble a little bit.

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:23.810
 What, and what do you mean when you say that the bursting in the bubble, like you were talking

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:25.990
 about before with the class divide or?

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:33.550
 Yeah, I mean, I mean that, I think that in Mexico city, um, it's easy because it's so

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:41.730
 large, I think that actually the, the social groups become very small, um, and geographically

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:48.190
 based because you can't easily move across the entire city, uh, every day.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:48.570
 Right.

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:56.920
 So, so I think that, um, it, like in every city, I think that it can be easy to get into

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:58.620
 this navel gazing space.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:04.970
 So, uh, I think it's good to find ways to remind people to, to go outside.

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:11.880
 And, uh, and, and, and, you know, and maybe people are reading the news or maybe people

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:15.560
 are interacting with other realities through other means, but I think that sometimes it's

00:38:15.560 --> 00:38:15.800
 good.

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:23.170
 It is a, it's a nice way to, in a very visceral way to get people to think about things and,

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:27.150
 and you hope that you can make them think about things through the food.

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:28.710
 Yeah.

00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:30.920
 I think that, I think that's well put.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:36.610
 I think there's a lot to say about that in the context of Mexico and everywhere.

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:43.790
 But, um, how do we, what's next for you and how can we follow along with what you're doing?

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:49.040
 Um, well, what is next for me?

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:55.010
 I, I'm now consulting on another little restaurant project, which is something that, uh,

00:38:55.230 --> 00:39:01.310
 now we've gone into the world of consulting and catering and things that are a little less,

00:39:01.310 --> 00:39:04.510
 um, demanding than constantly putting on events.

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:11.740
 So, you know, we, we, we try to do events open to the public, um, every month, every two months.

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:18.900
 Um, but now we're, we're entering to like a more personalized zone of consulting and, and

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:23.480
 almost work, uh, behind the scenes work for other restaurants.

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:26.290
 Uh, but we'll see, we'll see what happens out.

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:29.990
 Um, I have some good, really talented friends working on a website for us.

00:39:30.070 --> 00:39:36.130
 So hopefully we'll be live by the end of the year and there, um, we'll be

00:39:36.130 --> 00:39:39.780
 providing updates as to what we're up to.

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:44.200
 Um, and that is peachon.mx.

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:46.910
 So you can find us there.

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:48.120
 Great.

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:54.170
 Well, make sure to include that in the, in the notes below, along with a few other things

00:39:54.170 --> 00:39:54.670
 about you.

00:39:55.870 --> 00:39:57.330
 Um, thanks so much, Nikki.

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:00.210
 It's really been nice to talk to you about everything you're up to.

00:40:01.960 --> 00:40:03.320
 Nice talking to you too.

